@*
    One way of saying "working on it", so six pages stop inventing their own (EV-46).

    Before this each page had its own spinner or, on two of them, nothing at all — the audit
    trail and the call sheets simply showed an empty table until the data arrived. An empty
    table is indistinguishable from "there is nothing here", which is the wrong answer and
    the one a reader will believe.

    Two shapes, because two things are being waited on:

      Skeleton  a list that is coming. Keeps the page's shape so it does not jump when the
                rows land, and reads as structure rather than as emptiness.
      Spinner   an action in flight. There is nothing to reserve space for yet.

    **Screen readers are the point of `Label`, not decoration.** `role="status"` with
    `aria-live="polite"` announces the label when it appears, so a reader who cannot see the
    shimmer is told what is happening. `aria-busy` marks the region as not-yet-settled. The
    old markup had `role="status"` on the spinner and a visually-hidden word, which announced
    "Loading…" with no clue as to what — worse than silence on a page with three of them.
*@

<div class="busy @(Rows > 0 ? "busy--skeleton" : "busy--spinner")"
     role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-busy="true">

    @if (Rows > 0)
    {
        <span class="visually-hidden">@Label</span>
        @for (var row = 0; row < Rows; row++)
        {
            <div class="busy-row" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="busy-bar" style="width: @WidthFor(row)"></span>
            </div>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="busy-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <p class="busy-label">@Label</p>
    }

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Hint))
    {
        <p class="busy-hint">@Hint</p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What is being waited on, in words a producer would use. Announced to screen readers,
    /// and shown next to the spinner. "Loading…" is not a label; "Asking Gemini" is.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "Working…";

    /// <summary>Number of skeleton rows. Zero or less renders a spinner instead.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Rows { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Shown under the label for anything that takes long enough that a user starts to
    /// wonder. Saying "this takes about twenty seconds" is the difference between waiting
    /// and reloading.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Hint { get; set; }

    // Uneven widths, because a stack of identical bars reads as a graphic and a ragged one
    // reads as text that has not arrived.
    private static readonly string[] Widths = ["92%", "78%", "86%", "64%", "88%", "72%"];

    private string WidthFor(int row) => Widths[row % Widths.Length];
}
